    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Powerful self-expression requires creative agency and a sense of identity. ElectriStitch supports children in learning technical fluency, embedded in the sewing and making practices of their own culture and community. Learners are prompted to create through the engaging story of Em and Sky, humanoid robots. Patchwork, our first kit, introduces electrical components using a magnetic patch surface and flexible movable electronic and fabric shapes.  \r\n\r\nClothing and the things we wear communicate and tell others about who we are. We have found that children strongly place value in wearable things. Ultimately, ElectriStitch is centered around self-expression. We define self-expression as a product of identity and creative agency. We see identity as the representations of ourselves that we put into the world (Goffman, 1959). For children this often includes defining themselves through family, friends, things that they are good at, and things that they love (Erikson, 1968). We have identified self-expression as a need for children and ElectriStitch aims to engage children in reflective and intentional creating. \r\n\r\nIn needfinding of makerspaces and discussions around projects in maker-centered learning I took notice to the outcomes of and process of maker-centered learning. There are projects in makerspaces such as making a keychain that while utilizing fabrication technology and maker methods fall short in impact. Identity formation is seen as an outcome of maker-centered learning and making can be even more powerful when you are able to use your own experiences in making (Clapp et al., 2017). An open question for me became how can we bring ourselves to the forefront of making? With this question I quickly realized that for many the things that they wear are the center of their identities. \r\n\r\nWith the rise of the maker movement came a divide in the opportunities to engage with making and an elevation in the status of making that can be naturally observed in many communities. This particular problem persists because of access and equity to maker-centered learning opportunities. Maker technologies are expensive and can require curriculum and appropriate instruction. In formal educational environments there is a shortage of money to build maker opportunities and lack of teacher training and support. Electronic textiles are part of a larger movement to democratize making, by tapping into familiar practices in communities such as crafting and sewing. Working with electronic textiles exposes learners to powerful ideas in computation, sewing, and electronics. By making learners are able to practice and develop creative agency, which is the capacity and ability to create. By practicing creative agency learners are able to see themselves as agents of change (Clapp et al., 2017).",
